tel 1.0

base harlequin
import soundness

info   Scala and Java source highlighted by the Scala compiler itself, with types, diagnostics and completions
detail
    _Harlequin_ highlights Scala (and Java) source by running the real compiler over it,
    rather than approximating the language with regular expressions. The tokenizer and
    parser classify every token exactly as the compiler reads it — soft keywords are
    keywords only in position, interpolated strings are strings, a `val`'s name is a
    binding and a later use of it a usage — and, when a compiler and classpath are in
    scope, the whole frontend runs too, so each identifier carries the *type* the compiler
    gave it, the compiler's diagnostics come back located in the source, and a caret
    position turns highlighting into completion.

    The result of `Scala.highlight` is a `SourceCode`: a grid of lines, each a `List` of
    `Token`s, where a token has its text, an `Accent` (the colour category a palette maps
    to a colour), an optional `Role` (binding or usage), its position as a `Span`, and,
    at the deeper levels, a `Token.Meta` holding its type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val code: SourceCode = Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)")
    code.lines.to[List].flat.map(_.accent)
    // Keyword, Unparsed, Term, Unparsed, Symbol, Unparsed, Term, Parens, Number, …
    ```

    How deep the highlighting goes is decided by the `Highlight` given in scope, one of
    three in the `highlighting` package: `tokenizedScala`, the default (it is also
    `Highlight.default`, so `Scala.highlight` works with no import at all), needs nothing but
    the text; `typecheckedScala` and `compiledScala` need an anthology `Scalac` and a
    hellenism `LocalClasspath` as givens, and `sessionScala` a warm compiler session's
    `Scalac.Session.Frontend`. These are imported by name, since `import highlighting.*`
    would not import the givens.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
    given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
    import highlighting.typecheckedScala

    val typed = Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)")
    typed.lines.to[List].flat.seek(_.text == t"xs").let(_.meta).let(_.tpe.qualified)
    // scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Int]
    ```

    The library is split into source sets by what each needs:

    - `harlequin.core` — `SourceCode`, `Token`, `Accent`, `Role`, `ProgrammingLanguage`
      with `Scala` and `Java`, `Highlight` and `Depth`, `Diagnostic`, `Fragment`, `Lexis`,
      `Completion`/`Completions` (re-exported from prophesy) and `highlighting.tokenizedScala`.
      It embeds the Scala compiler, so it is JVM-only.
    - `harlequin.typed` — `highlighting.typecheckedScala`, `highlighting.compiledScala` and
      `highlighting.sessionScala`, which run an anthology `Scalac` against a hellenism
      `LocalClasspath`.
    - `harlequin.ansi` — `ScalaSyntaxPalette` and the `syntaxHighlighting` givens which
      render a `SourceCode` as escapade `Teletype`, with or without line numbers.
    - `harlequin.md` — `CommonFormattable`, the HTML rendering of fenced code blocks,
      behind punctuation's `formattables.scalaFormattable` and `formattables.javaFormattable`.
    - `harlequin.compiler` — the `Shim` over the compiler-internal API which differs between
      the Scala 3.9 and 3.10 streams; not for direct use.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella except the names which clash there:
    `Fragment` belongs to honeycomb and `Completion`/`Completions` to exoskeleton in the
    umbrella, so harlequin's are reached as `harlequin.Fragment`, `harlequin.Completion` and
    `harlequin.Completions`; likewise `syntaxHighlighting.tokenTeletypeable` is only
    `harlequin.syntaxHighlighting.tokenTeletypeable`. The `highlighting`,
    `syntaxHighlighting` and `formattables` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own.

refer harlequin.SourceCode
refer harlequin.Token
refer harlequin.Scala
refer harlequin.Highlight
refer harlequin.highlighting
refer harlequin.syntaxHighlighting

entry .SourceCode   highlighted source: a grid of accented tokens with diagnostics and completions
  detail
      A `SourceCode` is what `Scala.highlight` and `Java.highlight` (and `SourceCode.apply`
      directly) produce: `#lines`, an array of lines each a `List[Token]`, numbered from
      `#offset` (which is `1` for a freshly highlighted text, and the first line's number for
      a `#fragment`); the `#diagnostics` a typechecked or compiled run collected; and, when a
      caret was given, the `#completions` at it. A `#focus` span, set by `#extract`, is what
      the numbered terminal renderer underlines.

      Each line's tokens cover the line's text exactly, in order, with whitespace and comments
      as `Accent.Unparsed` tokens, so joining a line's token texts gives the line back. The
      line-ending newlines are not tokens. Tabs are rendered as two spaces.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val code = Scala.highlight(t"def twice(n: Int): Int =\n  n * 2")
      code.offset              // 1
      code.lastLine            // 2
      code(2).map(_.text).join // t"  n * 2"
      code.lines.to[List].flat.filter(_.role == Role.Binding).map(_.text)   // twice, n
      ```

      Which depth of highlighting produced it is decided by the `Highlight` given at the
      call: at `Depth.Tokenized` no token has a `Token.Meta` and `#diagnostics` is empty; at
      `Depth.Typechecked` and `Depth.Compiled` each identifier the typer resolved carries its
      type, and the diagnostics are the compiler's. A compile error never makes highlighting
      fail: the source highlights *with* its errors.
  refer harlequin.Token
  refer harlequin.Scala
  refer harlequin.Highlight
  refer harlequin.Diagnostic
  entry .apply   highlights text in a language, at the depth of the `Highlight` in scope
      `SourceCode(language, text, caret)(using Highlight)` is the engine behind
      `ProgrammingLanguage#highlight`, which is the usual way to call it. It tokenizes the
      text with the compiler's scanner (the Java scanner for `Java`), overlays the parse
      tree to decide which identifiers are terms and which types, and which of each are
      bindings; at `Depth.Typechecked` or `Depth.Compiled` it also runs the frontend of the
      `Highlight.Compilation` to collect types, elaborations and diagnostics, and, when a
      `caret` is given, completions.

      Soft modifiers (`inline`, `opaque`, `open`, `transparent`, `infix`, `erased`,
      `tracked`, `using`, …) are accented `Modifier` only where they are modifiers — before
      a hard keyword — and `Term` elsewhere, as the parser treats them. An unterminated
      string or character literal at the end of the input, which the scanner splits into an
      error token and a spilled last character, is folded back into one `Error` token, so
      an in-progress literal highlights as a unit.

      The tokenized pipeline runs with the compiler's `-source future` and no classpath, so
      it needs nothing but the text; a `Highlight` is a contextual parameter, and
      `Highlight.default` (tokenized) is used when none is imported.
  entry #language   the language the source was highlighted as: `Scala` or `Java`
  entry #offset   the number of the first line: `1` for a whole text, or a fragment's first line
      Line numbers are what the numbered terminal renderer prints and what `#apply`,
      `#extract` and `#fragment` take.
  entry #lines   the lines, each a `List[Token]` covering the line's text exactly
      An `Array[List[Token]]`, indexed from zero; `#apply` indexes it by line number
      instead. Newlines are not tokens, so a line's token texts joined are the line.
  entry #focus   a span of interest, if any, which the numbered renderer marks
      `Unset` unless set by `#extract` or `#fragment`. `syntaxHighlighting.numberedTeletypeable`
      draws a caret (`╱╲`) or underline (`‾‾‾`) beneath a single-line focus and a `║` in the
      margin of the lines a multi-line one covers.
  entry #diagnostics   the compiler's diagnostics, empty at `Depth.Tokenized`
      Each `Diagnostic` has an offset-mode `Span` into the text, its message and its
      `Diagnostic.Importance`. At `Depth.Typechecked` they are the frontend's; at
      `Depth.Compiled` the later phases' too.
  entry #completions   the completions at the caret, or `Unset` if no caret was given
      A `Completions` is the span of text a chosen completion replaces and its `Completion`
      items. With a caret, keyword completions are always offered, at any depth, from
      prophesy's pattern tree over the tokens before the caret; with a compiler in scope,
      the compiler's own completions — members, in-scope names, types in a type position,
      and a `Dynamic` receiver's members from its `Completable` companion — are merged in.
      See `Scala` for examples.
  entry #lastLine   the number of the last line: `offset + lines.length - 1`
  entry #apply   the tokens of a line, by its number
      `code(n)` is the line numbered `n` counting from `#offset`, so `code(code.offset)` is
      the first line and `code(code.lastLine)` the last. A number outside that range is an
      error.
  entry #extract   the lines around a span, as a fragment focused on it
      Takes a `Span` in line mode (such as a `Token#span`) and gives the `#fragment` from its
      first line to one past its last (two past, for a multi-line span), with the span as
      the `#focus`, so a diagnostic or a token can be shown in context.
  entry #fragment   the lines from one number to another, as a `SourceCode` of its own
      `code.fragment(startLine, endLine, focus)` keeps the lines numbered `startLine` to
      `endLine` inclusive, with `#offset` set to `startLine` so their numbers are preserved,
      and `focus` as the new `#focus`. Diagnostics and completions are not carried over.

entry .Token   one token of highlighted source: its text, accent, position, role and type
  detail
      A token is the unit a `SourceCode` is made of: a piece of `#text` which never
      contains a newline, the `#accent` which classifies it, a `#span` locating it, and,
      for a term or type identifier, its `#role` as a binding or a usage. At typechecked or
      compiled depth an identifier the typer resolved also has a `#meta` holding its type as
      a stenography `Syntax` and, on a callee, what the typer elaborated at the call.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val token = Token(t"xs", Accent.Term, role = Role.Binding)
      token.show      // t"xs"
      token.inspect   // Token(text:t"xs" ╱ accent:Term ╱ meta:○ ╱ span:⟪∅⟫ ╱ role:｢Binding｣)
      ```

      Two tokens are equal when all five fields are, which is how `Token.Newline` is found in
      a stream. A token is `Showable` as its text alone, which is what a rendered listing
      needs, and `Inspectable` with every field.
  refer harlequin.Accent
  refer harlequin.Role
  refer harlequin.SourceCode
  entry .Newline   the token which separates lines while a source is being assembled
      `Token("\n", Accent.Unparsed)`. It never appears in a finished `SourceCode`'s lines,
      which are split on it.
  entry .showable   a token shows as its text
  entry .inspectable   a token inspects with every field, in product form
      `Token(text:t"val" ╱ accent:Keyword ╱ meta:○ ╱ span:⟪∅⟫ ╱ role:○)`. A present `#meta` is
      shown as `｢Meta｣` without its type, since rendering a `Syntax` needs an `Imports`
      context which inspection cannot supply; a present role as `｢Binding｣` or `｢Usage｣`.
  entry .Meta   what the typer knows about a token: its type, and any elaboration of its call
    detail
        Attached to a token only at `Depth.Typechecked` or `Depth.Compiled`, and only to
        the identifiers the typer gave a type: a `val`/`def` name carries its declared or
        inferred type, a selection or identifier its type, a type tree its type. Tokens the
        compiler could not type (because of an error nearby, or a type stenography cannot
        render) simply have no `Meta`; highlighting never fails on one.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
        given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
        import highlighting.typecheckedScala

        val tokens = Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)").lines.to[List].flat
        tokens.seek(_.text == t"xs").let(_.meta).let(_.tpe.qualified)
        // scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Int]
        tokens.seek(_.text == t"List").let(_.meta).let(_.elaboration).let(_.typeArguments)
        // the inferred type argument, Int
        ```
    entry #tpe   the token's type, as a stenography `Syntax`
        Rendered with `Syntax#text` (abbreviated against an `Imports` in scope) or
        `Syntax#qualified`.
    entry #elaboration   what the typer added at this call site, if anything
        Present on a callee token whose call the typer elaborated beyond its source text: a
        `prophesy.Elaboration` listing the inferred type arguments and the synthesized `using`
        arguments, each naming the given resolved. Arguments written explicitly are never
        recorded.
  entry #text   the token's text, never containing a newline
  entry #accent   the colour category: keyword, term, type, number, string, symbol, …
  entry #meta   the typer's knowledge of the token, or `Unset` at tokenized depth
  entry #span   where the token is: a line-mode `Span` of 0-based line, column and length
      `Span.empty` for a token made by hand; a token in a `SourceCode` has its line and
      column, read as `span.startLine`, `span.startColumn` and `span.endColumn`, each an
      `Ordinal`.
  entry #role   `Role.Binding` or `Role.Usage` for a term or type identifier, else `Unset`
      Decided from the parse tree, so it is present at every depth: a `val`, `def`,
      parameter, pattern or type definition's name is a binding, and an identifier referring
      to one is a usage. Keywords, literals and punctuation have no role.
  entry #length   the length of the token's text
  entry #snip   the token split in two at a character offset
      `token.snip(point)` is the pair of tokens with the text before and from `point`, each
      keeping the accent, meta, span and role of the original.

entry .Accent   the colour category of a token, which a palette maps to one colour
  detail
      An accent is a colour category and nothing more. `Error` is a token the scanner could
      not read (an unterminated literal, say); `Number` a numeric literal; `String` a string,
      character or interpolated-string literal; `Term` a term identifier, including symbolic
      operators such as `+` and `::`, which Scala treats as identifiers; `Typal` a type
      identifier; `Keyword` a hard keyword; `Modifier` a modifier keyword (`private`,
      `lazy`, `override`, and a soft modifier such as `inline` where it is one); `Symbol` a
      punctuation token such as `=`, `=>`, `:` or `,`; `Parens` a bracket; and `Unparsed`
      whitespace, comments and anything else the scanner skipped.

      Whether a `Term` or `Typal` token defines a name or uses one is a separate annotation,
      `Role`, so a `val`'s name and a use of it share one accent and a styling policy may add
      italic or bold to bindings on top of the colour. In the terminal, `ScalaSyntaxPalette`
      gives each accent its colour; in HTML, `CommonFormattable` makes each accent a CSS class
      of the same name in lower case.

      An accent shows as its name in lower case: `Accent.Keyword.show == t"keyword"`.
  entry .showable   an accent shows as its lower-case name

entry .Role   whether a term or type token binds a name or refers to one
  detail
      `Binding` is the name being defined — a `val`, `var`, `def` or parameter name, a
      pattern variable (including each name in a tuple pattern, a `case` binder or a
      `for`-comprehension generator), or a class, trait, object, type or type-parameter
      name — and `Usage` is an identifier referring to a name bound elsewhere. It is decided
      from the parse tree alone, so it is present at tokenized depth, and it is `Unset` on a
      token which is neither a term nor a type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val tokens = Scala.highlight(t"def gg[TT](vv: TT) = vv").lines.to[List].flat
      tokens.filter(_.role == Role.Binding).map(_.text)   // gg, TT, vv
      tokens.filter(_.role == Role.Usage).map(_.text)     // TT, vv
      ```

      A role shows as its name in lower case.
  entry .showable   a role shows as its lower-case name

entry .ProgrammingLanguage   a language harlequin can highlight: `Scala` or `Java`
  detail
      A sealed trait with two instances, the objects `Scala` and `Java`. Its `#highlight`
      method is the entry point to the library, and its `#Context` type lets a language say
      what surrounds a fragment — a Scala term or type, rather than a whole file — so that a
      fragment which is not a complete compilation unit still parses.
  refer harlequin.Scala
  refer harlequin.Java
  refer harlequin.SourceCode
  entry #Context   what a fragment of this language may be embedded in
      `Scala.Context` is `Top`, `Term` or `Type`; `Java.Context` is `Top` only.
  entry #highlight   highlights text in this language, at the depth of the `Highlight` in scope
      `language.highlight(text, context, caret)(using Highlight)`: wraps the text as its
      `context` requires (`#preprocess`), highlights it with `SourceCode.apply`, and strips
      the wrapping from the result (`#postprocess`). `context` defaults to `Unset`, a
      complete compilation unit, and `caret`, an `Ordinal` offset into the text, to `Unset`,
      meaning no completions are computed. The `Highlight` given decides the depth;
      `Highlight.default` is tokenized, so no import is needed for plain highlighting.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)")
      Scala.highlight(t"List[Int]", Scala.Context.Type)
      Java.highlight(t"int n = 1;")
      ```
  entry #preprocess   the text a language wraps around a fragment before highlighting it
      Identity for a whole compilation unit (`Unset` or `Top`); `Scala` wraps a `Term` in a
      `val` and a `Type` in a `type` alias.
  entry #postprocess   the removal of the wrapping from the highlighted result
      Drops the lines `#preprocess` added, so the result's lines are the fragment's own.

entry .Scala   the Scala language: `Scala.highlight`, and the contexts a Scala fragment may be in
  detail
      The `ProgrammingLanguage` for Scala 3. `Scala.highlight(text)` tokenizes the text with
      the compiler's own scanner and parser; with a compiler in scope (`highlighting.
      typecheckedScala` or `compiledScala`, needing a `Scalac` and a `LocalClasspath`, or
      `sessionScala`, needing a `Scalac.Session.Frontend`) it runs the frontend too, so each
      identifier knows its type, and `compiledScala` runs the later phases as well, for the
      diagnostics only they produce.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Tokenized
        code
            locally:
              import highlighting.tokenizedScala
              val code = Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)")
              code.lines.to[List].flat.seek(_.text == t"xs").let(_.meta)   // Unset
              code.diagnostics                                            // Nil
      alternative Typechecked
        code
            locally:
              given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
              given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
              import highlighting.typecheckedScala
              val code = Scala.highlight(t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)")
              code.lines.to[List].flat.seek(_.text == t"xs").let(_.meta).let(_.tpe.qualified)
              // scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Int]
      alternative Compiled
        code
            locally:
              given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
              given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
              import highlighting.compiledScala
              Scala.highlight(t"val n: Int = \"oops\"").diagnostics.size   // 1
      ##
      ```

      A fragment which is not a complete compilation unit — a bare expression, or a type —
      is highlighted in a `Scala.Context`, which wraps it so that it parses and unwraps the
      result:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Scala.highlight(t"xs.map(_ + 1)", Scala.Context.Term)
      Scala.highlight(t"Map[Text, List[Int]]", Scala.Context.Type)
      ```

      A `caret` offset turns highlighting into completion. Keyword completions come from the
      tokens before the caret and need no compiler: `va` at the start of a statement offers
      `val` and `var`, `xs match ` offers `case`, and a position which must be a fresh name
      (after `val `) or a type offers no keywords at all. With a compiler in scope the
      compiler's interactive engine adds members after a `.`, in-scope names for a bare
      identifier, and only types, modules and packages in a type position.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val typed = t"val x = 1\nva"
      Scala.highlight(typed, caret = typed.length.z).completions.let(_.items.map(_.name))
      // val, var
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
      given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      import highlighting.typecheckedScala

      val source = t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)\nval y = xs.m"
      Scala.highlight(source, caret = source.length.z).completions.let(_.items.map(_.name))
      // map, max, mkString, … — the members of List[Int]
      ```

      A member of a `scala.Dynamic` receiver has no symbol to complete, so when the caret
      sits on a selection from a `Dynamic` type, the type's companion is loaded in this JVM
      and, if it implements `prophesy.Completable`, asked which members the receiver's
      refined type admits.
  refer harlequin.ProgrammingLanguage
  refer harlequin.SourceCode
  refer harlequin.highlighting
  entry .Context   what a Scala fragment is: a whole unit, a term, or a type
      `Top` is a complete compilation unit, the same as giving no context. `Term` wraps the
      fragment as the body of a `val` so that a bare expression parses, and `Type` as the
      right-hand side of a `type` alias, so that `Map[Text, Int]` highlights as a type;
      the wrapping lines are removed from the result in both cases.

entry .Java   the Java language: `Java.highlight`, tokenized with the compiler's Java scanner
  detail
      The `ProgrammingLanguage` for Java. `Java.highlight(text)` tokenizes with the Scala
      compiler's Java scanner, so Java keywords, literals and identifiers get their accents;
      only tokenized depth applies, since the Scala frontend does not type Java. Its one
      `Java.Context`, `Top`, is a whole compilation unit.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Java.highlight(t"public static void main(String[] args) {}")
      ```
  refer harlequin.ProgrammingLanguage
  entry .Context   the one context a Java fragment can be in: `Top`, a whole unit

entry .Highlight   how deep highlighting goes, and the compiler it runs: the given at every `highlight` call
  detail
      A `Highlight` is the contextual value `Scala.highlight` and `SourceCode.apply` take:
      its `#depth` says whether to tokenize only, to run the typer, or to run the later
      phases too, and its `#compilation` describes the compiler to run when the depth needs
      one. The instances are the givens of the `highlighting` package, imported by name:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import highlighting.tokenizedScala
      ```

      `Highlight.default` is the tokenized one and lives in the companion, so it is found
      with no import; a by-name import of `typecheckedScala`, `compiledScala` or
      `sessionScala` outranks it. A custom `Highlight` is a value with a `Depth` and a
      `Highlight.Compilation`, bound as a local given.
  refer harlequin.highlighting
  refer harlequin.Depth
  entry .default   the default highlighting, `highlighting.tokenizedScala`, in implicit scope
      Found through the companion whenever no other `Highlight` is in lexical scope, so
      `Scala.highlight(text)` needs no import for tokenized highlighting.
  entry .Compilation   what typechecked or compiled highlighting needs of a compiler
    detail
        The arguments, classpath and compiler instance a frontend run needs, abstracted so
        that `harlequin.core` knows nothing of anthology or hellenism: `harlequin.typed`
        builds one from a `Scalac` and a `LocalClasspath`, or from a warm session's
        `Scalac.Session.Frontend`. A tool with a compiler of its own can implement it
        directly.
    entry #arguments   the compiler's command-line arguments
    entry #classpath   the classpath, as one text in the platform's `-classpath` form
    entry #compiler   a fresh `dotty.tools.dotc.Compiler` to run
    entry #context   a context to type in, for a compilation which keeps a compiler warm
        Defaults to `Unset`, in which case a context is made afresh from `#classpath` and
        `#arguments` for every run, loading the classpath's symbol table each time. A warm
        session gives its base context instead: each run is given a fresh child of it, so
        what the session has already loaded and compiled is in scope.
  entry #depth   `Tokenized`, `Typechecked` or `Compiled`
  entry #compilation   the compiler to run, or `Unset` at `Tokenized` depth

entry .Depth   the three depths of highlighting: tokenized, typechecked, compiled
    `Tokenized` classifies tokens with the scanner and parser alone and needs no classpath.
    `Typechecked` runs the compiler frontend as far as the typer, so identifiers carry their
    types (`Token.Meta`), diagnostics are collected, and a caret yields the compiler's
    completions. `Compiled` additionally runs the phases after the typer (stopping before
    bytecode generation, so nothing is written) purely to surface the diagnostics only they
    can produce; the types are still read from the typer-stopped run, so they stay
    source-level. The `Highlight` given in scope carries the depth.

entry .highlighting   the `Highlight` givens, one per depth, each to import by name
  detail
      Which depth `Scala.highlight` works at is decided by the `Highlight` given in scope,
      and `import highlighting.*` would not import a given, so one is imported by name.
      `tokenizedScala` is also `Highlight.default`, so it applies with no import at all;
      the other three live in `harlequin.typed` and need a compiler.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
      given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      import highlighting.typecheckedScala

      Scala.highlight(t"val n: Int = \"oops\"").diagnostics.map(_.message)
      ```

      The `LocalClasspath` should be the one the code to highlight is written against:
      `LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[SomeClass])` for the classpath a running program's own
      classes came from.
  refer harlequin.Highlight
  refer harlequin.Depth
  entry .tokenizedScala   tokenized highlighting: accents and roles, no classpath, no compiler run
      `Depth.Tokenized` with no `Highlight.Compilation`. In `harlequin.core`, and also the
      companion's `Highlight.default`.
  entry .typecheckedScala   typechecked highlighting through a `Scalac` and a `LocalClasspath`
      `Depth.Typechecked`: runs the frontend of the given anthology `Scalac` with its
      command-line arguments over the given `LocalClasspath` (its directory and JAR entries,
      joined with the platform separator). Needs both as givens in scope. Each call makes a
      fresh compiler context and loads the classpath's symbol table, so highlighting many
      snippets against one classpath is faster through a session and `sessionScala`. In
      `harlequin.typed`.
  entry .compiledScala   compiled highlighting through a `Scalac` and a `LocalClasspath`
      As `typecheckedScala`, at `Depth.Compiled`: the post-typer phases also run, for the
      diagnostics only they produce. In `harlequin.typed`.
  entry .sessionScala   typechecked highlighting inside a warm compiler session
      `Depth.Typechecked`, run on the session's own compiler and in a child of its base
      context, from the `Scalac.Session.Frontend` given in scope — the value
      `compilation.frontend` gives inside `scalac.on(classpath).session:`. A snippet is typed
      against everything the session has compiled, without the classpath's symbol table
      being loaded again, so a REPL can highlight each line against its earlier definitions.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given (CompileEvent is Loggable) = Loggable.silent[CompileEvent]
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Scalac[3.8](Nil).on(LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])).session:
        compilation.compile(Map(t"earlier.scala" -> t"object Earlier:\n  val number: Int = 1\n"))
        given Scalac.Session.Frontend = compilation.frontend
        import highlighting.sessionScala

        Scala.highlight(t"val later = Earlier.number").diagnostics   // Nil: Earlier is in scope
      ```

      Two cautions: it must run on the session's thread, as the session's own compiles do
      (the compiler pins its context base to the thread of its first run); and since the
      snippet is typed as a source of the session, the top-level names it defines stay in
      scope there until the next snippet replaces them. The frontend is taken afresh for
      each use, since a session replaces its compiler when it crashes. In `harlequin.typed`.

entry .Diagnostic   a compiler diagnostic captured while highlighting: its span, message and importance
  detail
      What `SourceCode#diagnostics` holds, at `Depth.Typechecked` or `Depth.Compiled`. The
      `#span` is an offset-mode `Span` (a character offset into the text and a length),
      kept separate from the token stream because a diagnostic may cover part of a token or
      several tokens; a renderer reapplies it to the tokens it overlaps. Diagnostics without
      a position are dropped, and the compiler's own de-duplication of repeated messages at
      one position applies.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given Scalac[3.8, Universe.Classfile] = Scalac[3.8](Nil)
      given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      import highlighting.typecheckedScala

      Scala.highlight(t"val n: Int = \"oops\"").diagnostics.map: diagnostic =>
        (diagnostic.importance, diagnostic.span.offset, diagnostic.message)
      ```
  refer harlequin.SourceCode
  entry .Importance   the severity of a diagnostic: `Info`, `Warning` or `Error`
      Harlequin's own enum, named as anthology's `Importance` and larceny's are, so that
      `harlequin.core` does not depend on anthology.
  entry #span   where the diagnostic is: an offset-mode `Span` into the highlighted text
  entry #message   the compiler's message, as text
  entry #importance   `Info`, `Warning` or `Error`

entry .Completion   a completion candidate: a name, its kind and its signature
    Re-exported from prophesy (`prophesy.Completion`), since harlequin's completions are
    expressed in prophesy's types. A `Completion` has the `name` to insert, its `kind`
    (`Term`, `Method`, `Given`, `Extension`, `Type`, `Module`, `Package` or `Keyword`),
    its `signature` as a stenography `Syntax` (a method's full parameter and result
    signature, a value's type, or the keyword itself) and an optional `documentation`. In
    the `soundness` umbrella the name `Completion` belongs to exoskeleton, so this is
    reached as `harlequin.Completion`.

entry .Completions   the result of a completion request: the span to replace and the candidates
    Re-exported from prophesy (`prophesy.Completions`): `replace` is the offset-mode `Span`
    of the partial name a chosen completion replaces (empty at the caret when nothing has
    been typed yet), and `items` the `Completion`s, keywords first and sorted. It is what
    `SourceCode#completions` holds when a caret was given. In the `soundness` umbrella the
    name belongs to exoskeleton, so this is reached as `harlequin.Completions`.

entry .Fragment   analysis of a code fragment around a cursor, before any compiler runs
  detail
      The pure-text and tokenized splitting a completion host — a REPL, a debugger console —
      performs before deciding whether to invoke the compiler at all: where the partial
      identifier at the cursor begins, whether it selects a member, whether the token before
      it is a value awaiting an infix method, which brackets are left open, and where a
      line's depth-zero `:` or `=` is. None of it needs a classpath; `infixBase` tokenizes
      with the scanner, and the rest is text alone. Offsets are character offsets into the
      text, with the cursor just after the character at `offset - 1`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import harlequin.Fragment

      Fragment.memberBase(t"text.le", 7)            // (t"text.", t"le")
      Fragment.infixBase(t"hal(nym, sly) ma", 16)   // (t"hal(nym, sly).", t"ma")
      Fragment.unclosed(t"hal(nym, List[Int")       // List(']', ')')
      Fragment.groups(t"def f[T](a: T)(using c: C): T")   // List(t"a: T", t"using c: C")
      ```

      In the `soundness` umbrella the name `Fragment` belongs to honeycomb, so this is
      imported by name as `harlequin.Fragment`.
  refer harlequin.Lexis
  refer harlequin.Scala
  entry .identifierChar   whether a character may form part of an alphanumeric identifier
      A letter, a digit or `_`.
  entry .identifierStart   the offset at which the partial identifier ending at an offset begins
      Scans back over `identifierChar`s from `offset`; equal to `offset` when the cursor
      does not follow an identifier character.
  entry .memberBase   the member-selection base and partial name at the cursor
      `memberBase(code, offset)` splits the text at the cursor into the base — everything up
      to and including the `.` immediately before the partial member name — and the
      partial. The base is `Unset` when the cursor is not selecting a member (a first-token
      identifier, the first segment of an import), so there is no fixed type to enumerate
      against. `memberBase(t"text.length", 7)` is `(t"text.", t"le")`: the partial ends at
      the cursor, not the end of the input.
  entry .introducers   the keywords after which an identifier is a name, not a value
      `val`, `var`, `def`, `type`, `class`, `object`, `trait`, `enum`, `given`, `package`,
      `import`, `export`, `case`, `extension` and `new`: a name following one of these is
      being defined, imported or constructed, so it is not an infix receiver.
  entry .infixBase   the value receiver before a space and partial name, with a synthetic `.`
      When the cursor sits at `<value> <space> <partial>` — a value followed by whitespace,
      which is an infix method being typed — `infixBase(code, offset)` is the value
      expression with a trailing `.` added, so that the member-completion path serves it,
      and the partial name: `infixBase(t"xs ma", 5)` is `(t"xs.", t"ma")`. The base is
      `Unset` when there is no value receiver: the token before the space is a keyword
      (hard or soft: every Scala 3 keyword is rejected, so `erased ab` and `inline de` give
      no receiver), an operator, a comma or an open bracket, or a name in a definition or
      import position (`val xs ma`). A bracketed receiver is scanned as a balanced group,
      so `hal(nym, sly) ma` yields `hal(nym, sly).`.
  entry .expressionStart   where the value expression ending at the end of a text begins
      Scans back over identifier characters, `.` and balanced bracket groups, stopping at
      an operator, a space or an open bracket at depth zero: `expressionStart(t"a + hal.nym")`
      is `4`, and for `hal(nym).sly` it is `0`.
  entry .unclosed   the closing brackets for those left open, innermost first
      `unclosed(t"hal(nym, List[Int")` is `List(']', ')')`, so appending them in order
      completes the fragment. Brackets inside string and character literals (plain, escaped
      and triple-quoted) and inside line and block comments do not count; a closer with
      nothing open is ignored, so a fragment cut from inside a group still scans. The
      scanner does not see into an interpolation's `${…}`, which passes as string.
  entry .outermost   the offset of the first depth-zero character which satisfies a test
      `outermost(code, ':')` is the offset of the first `:` outside every bracket group,
      string and comment — the return type's colon in `def f(a: Int): String`, not a
      parameter's — or `Unset` if there is none; `outermost(code)(predicate)` tests each
      depth-zero character's offset instead, for a test such as "an `=` not followed by `>`".
  entry .split   the parts of a text between its depth-zero occurrences of a separator
      `split(t"a.*, b.{c, d}, e.given", ',')` is `a.*`, ` b.{c, d}` and ` e.given`: a
      separator inside brackets does not split. Parts are neither trimmed nor dropped when
      empty, so the separators' positions can be recovered; a text with no top-level
      separator is its own single part.
  entry .groups   the contents of each bracket group opened at depth zero, in order
      `groups(code)` gives the parameter clauses of a definition header without their
      brackets — `def f[T](a: T, b: (Int, Int))(using c: C): T` gives `a: T, b: (Int, Int)`
      and `using c: C` — and `groups(code, '[')` its type-parameter clauses. A group opened
      and never closed is omitted, being unfinished.

entry .Lexis   harlequin's tokens mapped onto prophesy's `Lexeme` alphabet, for keyword completion
  detail
      Keyword completion is grammatical: what may follow a caret depends on the tokens
      before it, not on any symbol table. `Lexis` maps a token stream onto prophesy's
      `Lexeme`s — the abstraction in which identifier text is irrelevant (a term identifier
      is just `Term`) but each keyword and symbol is significant, with `Break` at each
      statement boundary — and extracts the reversed context at a caret, which prophesy's
      `ScalaKeywords.pattern` looks up. The same mapping serves completion at runtime and
      the corpus analysis which derives the pattern tree, so the two always agree on what a
      context looks like. `Scala.highlight` with a caret uses it; a host which wants the
      context itself calls `context`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import harlequin.Lexis

      Lexis.context(t"val x = 1\nva", 12.z)
      // (t"va", List(Break, Literal, Symbol(=), Term, Keyword(val), Start))
      ```
  refer harlequin.Fragment
  entry .lexeme   the lexeme of one token, or `Unset` for whitespace
      Punctuation is classified by its text alone, never by its accent (a `(` is
      `Open(Round)`, a `=` a `Symbol`); a keyword or modifier by its text; a term identifier
      is `Term`, unless it is a soft modifier such as `inline` or `using`, which is a
      `Keyword` wherever it appears, since before a caret it cannot be told from an
      identifier; a type is `Typal`; numbers and strings are `Literal`; an `Unparsed` token
      contributes nothing.
  entry .lexemes   the whole lexeme sequence of a `SourceCode`, `Start` first
      With `Break` inserted at each line boundary which begins a new statement: a non-blank
      line indented at or below the previous non-blank line. A more deeply indented line
      continues its enclosing expression, so it contributes no boundary.
  entry .context   the partial identifier at a caret and the reversed lexeme context before it
      `context(text, caret, limit)` is the identifier fragment touching the caret (`Unset`
      when the caret does not follow an identifier character) and the lexemes preceding it,
      nearest first, at most `limit` (default 8) of them. The text before the fragment is
      tokenized afresh at tokenized depth, so no compiler runs. A caret on a blank line
      indented at or below the last significant line emits the `Break` the statement will
      have once it is typed.

entry .ScalaSyntaxPalette   a palette with one colour per accent, for rendering source in the terminal
    The type the `syntaxHighlighting` renderers take as a given: an iridescence `Palette`
    with `Form = Srgb`, refined with a colour for each `Accent` — `scalaError`,
    `scalaNumber`, `scalaString`, `scalaTerm`, `scalaType`, `scalaKeyword`, `scalaSymbol`,
    `scalaParenthesis`, `scalaModifier` and `scalaComment` (for `Unparsed` tokens, rendered
    in italic) — and three for the numbered renderer's margin: `margin` as its background,
    `accented` for the line numbers and `subdued` for the divider and the error marker.
    `Palette` itself requires `background` and `foreground`.

    No library ships a default, since a palette is a choice of colours; a program defines
    one as a given, filling the fields with iridescence colours:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label An iridescence palette, with the two colours every palette has
      code
          given palette: ScalaSyntaxPalette = new Palette:
            type Form = Srgb
            def background: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Black
            def foreground: Color in Srgb = WebColors.White
    frame
      label One colour per accent
      code
          given palette: ScalaSyntaxPalette = new Palette:
            type Form = Srgb
            def background: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Black
            def foreground: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.White
            def scalaError: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
            def scalaNumber: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gold
            def scalaString: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.LimeGreen
            def scalaTerm: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Silver
            def scalaType: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Turquoise
            def scalaKeyword: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
            def scalaSymbol: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gray
            def scalaParenthesis: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Gray
            def scalaModifier: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Orchid
            def scalaComment: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
    frame
      label And the three the numbered renderer's margin uses
      code
          given palette: ScalaSyntaxPalette = new Palette:
            type Form = Srgb
            def background: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Black
            def foreground: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.White
            def scalaError: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
            def scalaNumber: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gold
            def scalaString: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.LimeGreen
            def scalaTerm: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Silver
            def scalaType: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Turquoise
            def scalaKeyword: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
            def scalaSymbol: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gray
            def scalaParenthesis: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Gray
            def scalaModifier: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Orchid
            def scalaComment: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
            def subdued: Color in Srgb          = WebColors.DimGray
            def accented: Color in Srgb         = WebColors.White
            def margin: Color in Srgb           = WebColors.DimGray
    ##
    ```

    A palette maps each accent to a colour and nothing more: whether a token is a binding
    or a usage is its `Role`, and any further styling of bindings is a policy applied on
    top. The same palette serves punctuation's `scalaTeletypeFormattable` and delicious's
    `teletype` rendering of compiler messages. In `harlequin.ansi`.

entry .syntaxHighlighting   the terminal renderers of highlighted source, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Three escapade `Teletypeable` givens, in `harlequin.ansi`, each needing a
      `ScalaSyntaxPalette` given in scope. `numberedTeletypeable` and `unnumberedTeletypeable`
      both render a `SourceCode`, so exactly one is imported by name; `tokenTeletypeable`
      renders a single `Token`, and is what the other two use for each token.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Unnumbered
        code
            locally:
              import syntaxHighlighting.unnumberedTeletypeable
              val code = Scala.highlight(t"def twice(n: Int): Int =\n  n * 2")
              code.teletype
              // def twice(n: Int): Int =
              //   n * 2
      alternative Numbered
        code
            locally:
              import syntaxHighlighting.numberedTeletypeable
              val code = Scala.highlight(t"def twice(n: Int): Int =\n  n * 2")
              code.teletype
              // 1┋ def twice(n: Int): Int =
              // 2┋   n * 2
      ##
      ```

      Only `numberedTeletypeable` and `unnumberedTeletypeable` are mirrored in the
      `soundness` umbrella's `syntaxHighlighting` package; `tokenTeletypeable` is imported as
      `harlequin.syntaxHighlighting.tokenTeletypeable`.
  refer harlequin.ScalaSyntaxPalette
  refer harlequin.SourceCode
  entry .tokenTeletypeable   renders one `Token` in its accent's palette colour
      Each accent is rendered in the palette colour of the same name (`Error` in
      `scalaError`, `Parens` in `scalaParenthesis`, and so on), and an `Unparsed` token —
      whitespace and comments — in `scalaComment` and italic. The token's role is not
      styled. Needs a `ScalaSyntaxPalette`.
  entry .numberedTeletypeable   renders a `SourceCode` with a line-number margin, marking its focus
      Each line is prefixed by its number (from `SourceCode#offset`), right-aligned to the
      width of the last line's number, in the palette's `accented` colour on its `margin`
      background, followed by a `┋` divider in `subdued`. A `SourceCode#focus` is marked:
      a single-line focus of zero width gets a `╱╲` caret beneath its column, one of some
      width an underline of `‾`, both in `scalaError`; a focus spanning several lines marks
      the lines it covers with `║` in the margin. Needs a `ScalaSyntaxPalette`.
  entry .unnumberedTeletypeable   renders a `SourceCode` as its lines alone, coloured by accent
      The lines joined with newlines, each token in its accent's colour, with no margin and
      no focus marking. Needs a `ScalaSyntaxPalette`.

entry .CommonFormattable   renders a fenced code block as HTML, one `<code>` element per token
  detail
      The punctuation `Formattable` behind `formattables.scalaFormattable` and
      `formattables.javaFormattable`, in `harlequin.md`: a fenced block tagged `scala` or
      `java` in a Markdown document is highlighted with `Scala.highlight` or
      `Java.highlight` and rendered as `<div class="amok"><pre>…</pre></div>`, with each
      line a `<span class="line">` and each token a `<code>` whose classes are its accent and
      role. A stylesheet is the styling policy for this front-end: `.keyword` colours the
      keywords, and `.binding` can italicise bindings on top of their accent's colour.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formattables.scalaFormattable

      Parser.parse(t"```scala\nval xs = List(1, 2, 3)\n```").html
      ```

      A custom front-end for another language extends the trait with a `Self` type (the
      fence tag) and a `format` which highlights the content and passes the `SourceCode` to
      the trait's `postprocess`.
  refer harlequin.Accent
  refer harlequin.Role
  entry #lineClass   the given which allows `class="line"` and `class="amok"` on the elements
  entry #classes   the CSS classes of a token: its accent, and its role if it has one
      `classes(accent, role)` is a `ClassList` of the accent's lower-case name (`keyword`,
      `term`, `typal`, …) and, for a term or type identifier, `binding` or `usage`.
  entry #element   a token as `<code class="…">text</code>`
      `element(accent, role, text)` is the `code` element with `#classes` for its class
      attribute and the token's text as its content.

entry .Shim   the spelling of the compiler-internal API harlequin wraps, per compiler stream
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `harlequin.compiler` holds one `Shim` for the Scala 3.9 stream and
      one for 3.10, defining the same names against each stream's shapes, so that nothing
      else in harlequin knows which is in play.
  entry .interactiveDriver   an `InteractiveDriver` over compiler settings, for completions
      3.10 replaced the driver's `Option[LogicalPackage]` parameter with a
      `CachedLogicalPackage` with no default; the 3.9 shim omits it and the 3.10 shim passes
      `CachedLogicalPackage.none`, which is the same empty case.
